---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import TableOfContents from '../../components/sections/TableOfContents.astro';

// FAQ: real buyer questions, rendered as visible content and as FAQPage JSON-LD
const faq = [
	{
		question: 'Will a pre-action system discharge if a sprinkler head breaks by accident?',
		answer: 'That depends on which interlock the valve group is wired for, and it is the first thing to settle on the enquiry. On a double-interlock arrangement such as the ZSFY dual-signal design, two independent signals are required before the diaphragm chamber releases - typically a detection signal plus a pressure change from the piping. A dropped head, a holed pipe or a single faulty detector therefore cannot dump water on the assets below. The trade-off is time: nothing reaches the pipework until both conditions exist, so a real fire runs unopposed for those seconds. A single-interlock arrangement admits water as soon as detection operates, and a sprinkler operating first will also trip the valve, which behaves more like a dry-pipe system.'
	},
	{
		question: 'What pressure does the system side of a pre-action valve hold?',
		answer: 'The ZSFY group is supplied for a system-side air pressure of 0.03 - 0.05 MPa, the same low-pressure band used on the ZSFC dry alarm valve, with the water side held full at up to 1.6 MPa. That pressure exists for supervision, not for suppression: it is what lets a leak detection device prove the piping is intact before a fire, and what makes an open head or a rupture visible within minutes instead of at the next inspection. Because the margin between 0.05 MPa and a trip is small, the air side is held by an automatic maintenance device and watched by a low-pressure alarm switch set well above the trip point.'
	},
	{
		question: 'Which sizes and actuation options are available for the ZSFY?',
		answer: 'The catalogue range is DN80 - DN250 at 1.6 MPa nominal, with pneumatic, electric or manual trigger options and integrated pressure-maintaining and air-feeding devices in the group. Bodies and bonnets are ductile iron QT450-10, the internal clapper is bronze or spheroidal iron covered with EPDM, and the diaphragm chamber is reinforced NBR, with galvanized piping and copper-alloy trim. Where a riser needs DN300, the ZSFM deluge group covers DN50 - DN300 at the same working pressure, but it is a different valve type with open discharge points - substitute only with the engineer signing the change.'
	},
	{
		question: 'Do I need an exhaust accelerator on a pre-action system?',
		answer: 'Ask the question rather than assuming either answer. Water delivery time is the interval between the valve opening and water reaching the most remote operating head; on a pre-action or dry-pipe system that interval is dominated by how fast the air in the pipework can get out of the way. Large-volume piping, long runs, several stories of riser and high system air pressure all lengthen it. An exhaust accelerator senses the drop in air pressure and speeds the opening of the valve so water starts filling sooner, and where the project specifies a maximum delivery time it is usually the cheapest way to meet it. Confirm the required figure from the hydraulic calculation in your tender documents, then size the trim to it.'
	},
	{
		question: 'Why would a pre-action valve trip with no fire?',
		answer: 'Most nuisance trips trace back to the air side or the trim, not the main valve. The four common ones: system air leaking down faster than the maintenance device can replace it, so the pressure falls to trip level overnight; condensate frozen in a trim passage or drain during a cold snap, then released when it thaws; water logged in the priming chamber, which changes the balance the valve holds itself closed with; and a loss of supervisory power or a failed solenoid in an electric release circuit. Debris in the pilot passages belongs on the same list - a diaphragm valve held closed by pilot pressure is held closed by passages small enough to be blocked by the grit a main-line gate valve would pass without complaint, which is why a strainer on the pilot supply is worth specifying on the same order.'
	},
	{
		question: 'Is a pre-action valve the same thing as a deluge valve?',
		answer: 'No, and mixing them up is a design error with expensive consequences. A pre-action system protects closed sprinkler heads and keeps the pipework empty and air-filled, so water is admitted only after an interlock condition and then has to fill the network before the heads discharge. A deluge system protects open heads or monitors: the piping is permanently empty, and when the deluge valve opens, every discharge point in the protected area releases at once. Pre-action is chosen to prevent accidental water discharge; deluge is chosen to flood a hazard simultaneously. Because the pipework, the discharge elements and the detection logic all differ, the two should never be treated as interchangeable line items on a tender.'
	}
];

// TOC: one entry per H2 id in the body
const toc = [
	{ id: 'what-the-system-is-actually-for', label: '1. What the Empty Pipe Is Buying You' },
	{ id: 'single-interlock-double-interlock-dry-pipe', label: '2. Single-Interlock, Double-Interlock and Dry-Pipe Pre-action' },
	{ id: 'how-the-zsfy-group-holds-and-trips', label: '3. How the ZSFY Group Holds Closed and Trips' },
	{ id: 'trim-and-supervision-around-the-valve', label: '4. Trim and Supervision Around the Valve' },
	{ id: 'sizing-air-side-and-hydraulic-checks', label: '5. Sizing, the Air Side and Hydraulic Checks' },
	{ id: 'pre-action-compared-with-wet-dry-and-deluge', label: '6. Pre-action Compared with Wet, Dry-Pipe and Deluge' },
	{ id: 'installation-freeze-protection-and-drains', label: '7. Installation, Freeze Protection and Drain Arrangement' },
	{ id: 'commissioning-and-periodic-testing', label: '8. Commissioning and Periodic Testing' },
	{ id: 'nuisance-trips-and-how-to-find-them', label: '9. Nuisance Trips and How to Find Them' },
	{ id: 'pre-action-valve-rfq-checklist', label: '10. Pre-action Valve RFQ Checklist' },
	{ id: 'faq', label: 'Frequently Asked Questions' }
];

// Related products: slugs match src/utils/products.ts
const products = [
	{ slug: 'pre-action-alarm-system-zsfy', name: 'ZSFY Pre-action Valve Group', img: '/assets/products/pre-action-alarm-system-zsfy.jpg', desc: 'DN80 - DN250, 1.6 MPa, dual-signal trigger, air side 0.03 - 0.05 MPa' },
	{ slug: 'dry-alarm-valve-zsfc', name: 'ZSFC Dry Alarm Valve', img: '/assets/products/dry-alarm-valve-zsfc.webp', desc: 'DN100 - DN200 PN16, air-operated clapper, delay chamber and gong' },
	{ slug: 'deluge-alarm-valve-zsfm', name: 'ZSFM Deluge Alarm Valve', img: '/assets/products/deluge-alarm-valve-zsfm.webp', desc: 'DN50 - DN300, DC24V / 0.5A solenoid pilot, open-head discharge duty' },
	{ slug: 'water-flow-indicator-zsjz', name: 'ZSJZ Water Flow Indicator', img: '/assets/products/water-flow-indicator-zsjz.png', desc: 'DN50 - DN200, 2-60 s delay, dry contact signal to the panel' }
];
---

<Layout
	title="Pre-Action Valve Guide: ZSFY Trim, Sizing & Trips"
	description="How a ZSFY pre-action valve works, single vs double interlock, air trim, DN80-DN250 sizing, commissioning, nuisance trips and the RFQ lines."
	type="article"
	faq={faq}
	breadcrumbs={[{ name: 'Guides', href: '/blog/' }, { name: 'Pre-Action Valve (ZSFY) Guide' }]}
>
	<main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-14 mb-16">
		<!-- Breadcrumb -->
		<nav class="text-xs text-gray-400 font-bold uppercase tracking-widest mb-6 flex gap-2">
			<a href="/" class="hover:text-blue-600">Home</a>
			<span>/</span>
			<a href="/blog/" class="hover:text-blue-600">Guides</a>
			<span>/</span>
			<span class="text-gray-600">Pre-Action Valve (ZSFY) Guide</span>
		</nav>

		<article class="bg-white rounded-2xl shadow-sm border border-gray-100 p-6 sm:p-10">
			<p class="text-brand-red font-bold uppercase tracking-widest text-sm mb-3">Sprinkler System Valve Guide</p>
			<h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-black text-gray-900 leading-tight tracking-tight">Pre-Action Valve (ZSFY) Selection Guide: Interlock, Trim, Sizing and Nuisance Trips</h1>
			<p class="mt-5 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed text-lg">In short: a pre-action valve exists to keep water away from pipework that protects itself with air. The <a href="/products/pre-action-alarm-system-zsfy/" class="text-brand-blue font-semibold hover:underline">ZSFY group</a> holds the sprinkler network dry and pressurised with air at 0.03 - 0.05 MPa while the supply side stays full at 1.6 MPa, and it admits water only when the interlock it is wired for is satisfied - which is why it is specified for archives, museums, data halls and cold spaces where an accidental discharge costs more than the fire. Whether that delay is acceptable, which trim the valve actually needs, and what has to be written on the purchase order are the subjects below. This guide is about the valve and its trim; the decision between wet, dry and pre-action as a system type is covered in the <a href="/guides/fire-sprinkler-system-wet-dry-preaction/" class="text-brand-blue font-semibold hover:underline">sprinkler system type guide</a>.</p>

			<TableOfContents headings={toc} />

			<!-- Product strip -->
			<div class="grid grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-4 gap-4 mb-4">
				{products.map(p => (
					<a href={`/products/${p.slug}/`} class="group bg-gray-50 rounded-xl border border-gray-100 overflow-hidden hover:border-brand-red hover:shadow-md transition">
						<img src={p.img} alt={p.name} loading="lazy" class="w-full h-28 object-contain bg-white p-2" />
						<div class="p-3">
							<p class="text-xs font-bold text-gray-800 group-hover:text-brand-red leading-snug transition-colors">{p.name}</p>
							<p class="text-[10px] text-gray-400 mt-1 line-clamp-2">{p.desc}</p>
						</div>
					</a>
				))}
			</div>

			<!-- Section 1 -->
			<h2 id="what-the-system-is-actually-for" class="text-2xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-12 mb-4 tracking-tight scroll-mt-24">1. What the Empty Pipe Is Buying You</h2>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">Buyers usually discover pre-action protection through a claim rather than a design review. A wet system with a <a href="/products/wet-alarm-check-valve-zsfz-150/" class="text-brand-blue font-semibold hover:underline">ZSFZ wet alarm check valve</a> keeps water standing in every branch pipe, right up to the fusible element of each head. That is exactly what makes a wet system fast and exactly what makes it vulnerable: a corroded tee, an impact from a rack beam, a frozen section that cracks on thaw, or a head loosened during ceiling work turns the pipework into a leak with a valve full open upstream. Nothing about that failure requires a fire.</p>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">Pre-action removes that exposure. Under normal conditions the pipework carries air at a low supervisory pressure - 0.03 - 0.05 MPa on this group - and the water is held back at the valve. The consequences for procurement are three:</p>
			<ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2 mb-4">
				<li><strong>Accidental discharge needs a second failure.</strong> Piping damage vents air, and how the valve responds to that venting is precisely what the interlock arrangement decides. This is the whole reason a records store or a switching room is specified pre-action rather than wet.</li>
				<li><strong>The air path becomes part of the scope.</strong> A pre-action system needs an air supply, an air maintenance arrangement, supervisory pressure switches, and pipework that is genuinely tight. Budget for the air side and the leak test, not only the valve.</li>
				<li><strong>Water delivery is no longer instantaneous.</strong> After the valve opens, the network has to exhaust its air and fill. That filling time is the price of the protection, and the trim, the pipe volume and the exhaust arrangement decide how large the price is.</li>
			</ul>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Cold spaces are the second family of projects. An unheated warehouse, a loading canopy, a cold-store envelope or an outdoor attachment in a freezing climate cannot hold water; the alternatives are air - or antifreeze, which most modern specifications avoid. Where the answer is air, the dry-pipe and pre-action chapters of the <a href="/guides/nfpa-fire-protection-standards-guide/" class="text-brand-blue font-semibold hover:underline">NFPA standards guide</a> and the <a href="/guides/ce-en12845-fire-protection-compliance/" class="text-brand-blue font-semibold hover:underline">CE and EN 12845 compliance guide</a> show how to trace the requirement back to the document governing your project.</p>

			<!-- Section 2 -->
			<h2 id="single-interlock-double-interlock-dry-pipe" class="text-2xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-12 mb-4 tracking-tight scroll-mt-24">2. Single-Interlock, Double-Interlock and Dry-Pipe Pre-action</h2>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">Three arrangements share the same empty-pipe principle and differ in what has to happen before water is admitted. They are not interchangeable, and the tender usually names one. The ZSFY is built as a dual-signal group - a fire alarm signal and a pressure signal both required - which puts it in the double-interlock family, but the same valve body is used in single-interlock and dry-pipe duty where the trim and detection logic are arranged for it. Confirm which arrangement the engineer expects before quoting trim components.</p>
			<div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-gray-200 my-5">
				<table class="min-w-full divide-y divide-gray-200 text-sm">
					<thead class="bg-gray-50">
						<tr>
							<th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-wider">What to check</th>
							<th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-wider">Single-Interlock</th>
							<th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-wider">Double-Interlock (ZSFY dual-signal)</th>
							<th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-wider">Dry-Pipe Pre-action</th>
						</tr>
					</thead>
					<tbody class="bg-white divide-y divide-gray-200">
						<tr>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold text-gray-900">What opens the valve</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Detection system operating alone</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Two independent signals - detection plus a pressure change</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Sprinkler operation, or detection, depending on the trim logic</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold text-gray-900">State of pipework when it opens</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Empty; it fills after the valve opens</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Empty until both signals exist</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Air-filled; a head operating first vents it</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold text-gray-900">Accidental discharge risk</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Reduced versus wet, but one detector fault can flood the zone</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Lowest - a single head break or single detector fault cannot open it</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Reduced versus wet; behaves like a dry system</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold text-gray-900">Time to water</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Fast of the three - piping is already charged before the head opens</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Slowest - fill starts only after both signals</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Dominated by air exhaust from the operating head</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold text-gray-900">Typical specification</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Occupied offices, retail, where water damage matters but response matters more</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Archives, museums, data halls, control rooms, heritage</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Unheated and cold spaces, refrigerated and freezer areas</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold text-gray-900">Detection dependency</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Highest - detection is the trigger</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Highest - detection is half the trip condition</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Lower when sprinkler operation alone is accepted</td>
						</tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</div>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Read the table against the building, not against the catalogue. A double-interlock arrangement in a warehouse where a fast-growing pallet fire doubles every two minutes is buying protection from water damage at the cost of suppression time, and few engineers accept that trade outside a genuine water-sensitive room. Where the trade is not worth making, a dry-pipe system on a <a href="/products/dry-alarm-valve-zsfc/" class="text-brand-blue font-semibold hover:underline">ZSFC dry alarm valve</a> gives the same freeze protection and the same empty pipework with less logic in the trip path.</p>

			<!-- Section 3 -->
			<h2 id="how-the-zsfy-group-holds-and-trips" class="text-2xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-12 mb-4 tracking-tight scroll-mt-24">3. How the ZSFY Group Holds Closed and Trips</h2>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">The valve is a diaphragm or clapper main valve held closed by a balance of pressures rather than by a spring or a handwheel. Body and bonnet are ductile iron QT450-10; the internal clapper is bronze or spheroidal iron covered with EPDM; the diaphragm chamber uses reinforced NBR; the trim is galvanized piping with copper-alloy fittings. Under normal conditions the supply side sits at system water pressure up to 1.6 MPa, the system side holds 0.03 - 0.05 MPa of air, and the trim holds a priming pressure that keeps the clapper seated. Nothing moves and nothing flows.</p>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">The trip sequence is a pressure story. Detection operates and the releasing device - pneumatic, electric or manual, all three are offered on this group - vents the holding pressure from the diaphragm chamber. Supply water then enters the chamber and lifts the clapper, water runs into pipework that until that moment held air, and the air is pushed out through the operating head, the exhaust accelerator if one is fitted, and the drains. As the water side moves, the alarm path reports it: pressure switch to the panel, water-motor gong locally, gauges showing the change at the valve. The integrated pressure-maintaining and air-feeding devices included in the group are what keep the air side at its supervisory value between trips and what stop ordinary weeping joints from draining it.</p>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">Two consequences follow that a buyer should carry into the quotation. First, the releasing circuit is an interface with someone else - the fire alarm panel, not the valve supplier. State the signal type and voltage you intend to use; the solenoid pilot on the related <a href="/products/deluge-alarm-valve-zsfm/" class="text-brand-blue font-semibold hover:underline">ZSFM deluge group</a> is specified as DC24V / 0.5A and the same discipline of naming coil voltage and supervision belongs here, as does the fail-safe behaviour you expect when the panel loses power. Second, a group held closed by pilot pressure is held closed by small passages. Grit in the trim is a trip or a leak waiting to happen, and the strainer question is dealt with in the <a href="/guides/y-strainer-selection-guide/" class="text-brand-blue font-semibold hover:underline">pipeline strainer guide</a>.</p>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">For the alarm and dry-pipe side of the same family, the <a href="/guides/dry-alarm-valve-zsfc-guide/" class="text-brand-blue font-semibold hover:underline">dry alarm valve guide</a> sets out how the ZSFC holds on air pressure and how its delay chamber and gong are arranged - the trim logic transfers almost line for line.</p>

			<!-- Section 4 -->
			<h2 id="trim-and-supervision-around-the-valve" class="text-2xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-12 mb-4 tracking-tight scroll-mt-24">4. Trim and Supervision Around the Valve</h2>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">The valve body is a fraction of the delivered scope. What makes a pre-action installation maintainable is everything bolted, piped and wired around it, and each item deserves a line on the enquiry rather than the phrase "complete trim".</p>
			<ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2 mb-4">
				<li><strong>Air maintenance and air supply.</strong> A compressor or cylinder set with the pressure-maintaining device keeps the supervisory air at its set band. Sizing follows the acceptable make-up volume, because an air maintenance device that cannot cover a legitimate leak will keep the system alive while hiding a growing hole.</li>
				<li><strong>Low-air pressure alarm.</strong> Set comfortably above the trip value so that falling air is an alarm a person can act on rather than a flood. This is the single most valuable device on an air-filled system.</li>
				<li><strong>Exhaust acceleration.</strong> Fitted where water delivery time is specified; see section 5 for how to decide.</li>
				<li><strong>Isolation and position supervision.</strong> The main control valve must be supervised open. A rising-stem <a href="/products/ul-fm-resilient-seat-osy-gate-valve/" class="text-brand-blue font-semibold hover:underline">OS&amp;Y gate valve</a> (DN50 - DN250, nodular cast iron, grooved ends) shows position at a glance; a <a href="/products/signal-grooved-wafer-butterfly-valve-dn80/" class="text-brand-blue font-semibold hover:underline">signal butterfly valve</a> (DN50 - DN300 PN16, wafer or grooved, waterproof microswitch) sends it to the panel, which is what a night-shift operator actually needs. The trade-off between the two is covered in the <a href="/guides/fire-sprinkler-control-valve-types/" class="text-brand-blue font-semibold hover:underline">sprinkler control valve types guide</a>.</li>
				<li><strong>Zone flow reporting.</strong> On a network of closed heads, a <a href="/products/water-flow-indicator-zsjz/" class="text-brand-blue font-semibold hover:underline">ZSJZ vane flow indicator</a> (DN50 - DN200, 1.2 or 1.6 MPa, trigger between 15 and 37.5 L/min, adjustable 2 - 60 second delay, dry contact output) tells the panel which zone is flowing, and its delay is what stops pressure oscillation from reporting as a fire.</li>
				<li><strong>Test, drip and drain connections.</strong> The trim needs a controlled way to prove the alarm path without emptying the protected volume, plus a drip path so a weeping seat shows as a drain discharge rather than as a mysterious air loss.</li>
				<li><strong>Heads.</strong> The discharge elements stay closed, and standard-response or quick-response <a href="/products/glass-bulb-fire-sprinkler-zst/" class="text-brand-blue font-semibold hover:underline">ZST glass bulb heads</a> (K=80 +/-4 or K=115 +/-9, 57 / 68 / 79 / 93 / 141 degrees C) or <a href="/products/esfr-sprinkler-head/" class="text-brand-blue font-semibold hover:underline">ESFR heads</a> on a 74 degrees C bulb are selected by the hazard - see the <a href="/guides/fire-sprinkler-head-selection-guide/" class="text-brand-blue font-semibold hover:underline">sprinkler head selection guide</a>.</li>
			</ul>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Where the project also needs a remote-acting valve for a monitor or a deluge section, the <a href="/products/groove-solenoid-control-gate-valve-gkzf1/" class="text-brand-blue font-semibold hover:underline">GKZF-1 grooved solenoid control gate valve</a> (DN50 - DN300, 300 PSI / 2.0 MPa, AC220V or DC24V) is a separate role and should not be substituted into an alarm valve group because it looks similar on a drawing.</p>

			<!-- Section 5 -->
			<h2 id="sizing-air-side-and-hydraulic-checks" class="text-2xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-12 mb-4 tracking-tight scroll-mt-24">5. Sizing, the Air Side and Hydraulic Checks</h2>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">Size is the easy part and the least interesting. The ZSFY covers DN80 - DN250 at 1.6 MPa, which takes most risers and zone duties; where a design needs above that, the deluge group covers DN50 - DN300 at the same working pressure with a 3.2 MPa test pressure and 6.4 MPa shell test. What actually needs checking on a pre-action installation is the behaviour of the air.</p>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4"><strong>Air volume and exhaust time.</strong> Total the internal volume of everything that has to be emptied of air before water arrives at the remote head. Compare that volume with the exhaust path available - the operating head orifice, the accelerator, the open drains - and you have the filling time that the design either accepts or does not. Ask the engineer for the maximum trip-to-water figure in the specification and quote the trim against it, rather than agreeing "pre-action" and discovering at commissioning that the acceptance test cannot be met.</p>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4"><strong>Leakage rate.</strong> The supervisory pressure band of 0.03 - 0.05 MPa is narrow, so the make-up volume demanded of the air maintenance device is the number that reveals the real condition of the pipework. Specify a measured leakage figure at a stated pressure as a commissioning acceptance item; it is the only objective evidence that the air side is fit to hold.</p>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4"><strong>Temperature swing.</strong> Air at 0.05 MPa moves with ambient temperature in a way water does not. A day-night swing in an uninsulated roof space can cycle supervisory pressure enough to nuisance-trip or nuisance-alarm. Shade the pipework where you can, and let the maintenance device absorb the rest.</p>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed"><strong>Water side capacity.</strong> Confirm the valve against the design flow and the available inlet pressure, and ask for Kv or Cv stated at a defined pressure drop together with the pressure-loss figure at your flow - the same discipline applies as in the <a href="/guides/deluge-valve-selection-guide/" class="text-brand-blue font-semibold hover:underline">deluge sizing chapter</a>. Add the remote head pressure and the riser height, and check the whole duty against the <a href="/guides/fire-pump-room-valves-guide/" class="text-brand-blue font-semibold hover:underline">pump room arrangement</a> so the supply and the valve are quoted for the same case. Where a project needs the numbers verified rather than asserted, an <a href="/products/smart-electromagnetic-flow-meter/" class="text-brand-blue font-semibold hover:underline">electromagnetic flow meter</a> on the test header turns the acceptance test into a record.</p>

			<!-- Section 6 -->
			<h2 id="pre-action-compared-with-wet-dry-and-deluge" class="text-2xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-12 mb-4 tracking-tight scroll-mt-24">6. Pre-action Compared with Wet, Dry-Pipe and Deluge</h2>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">Four alarm valve groups, four different failure modes, four different RFQs. The table below uses the catalogue figures for these valves so the comparison is verifiable rather than generic.</p>
			<div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-gray-200 my-5">
				<table class="min-w-full divide-y divide-gray-200 text-sm">
					<thead class="bg-gray-50">
						<tr>
