			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">If you are choosing between the four rather than pricing one, start from the hazard and the contents, then work down to the valve - that is the purpose of the <a href="/guides/fire-sprinkler-system-valves-guide/" class="text-brand-blue font-semibold hover:underline">sprinkler system valves guide</a> and the <a href="/guides/foam-deluge-system-guide/" class="text-brand-blue font-semibold hover:underline">foam deluge guide</a> for Class B risks. Where the tender lists pre-action and deluge as options, treat them as different systems rather than different valves and price the pipework, the heads and the detection accordingly.</p>

			<!-- Section 7 -->
			<h2 id="installation-freeze-protection-and-drains" class="text-2xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-12 mb-4 tracking-tight scroll-mt-24">7. Installation, Freeze Protection and Drain Arrangement</h2>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">A pre-action valve installed like a wet alarm valve will disappoint on the first cold snap. Three installation decisions carry most of the risk.</p>
			<ol class="list-decimal pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2 mb-4">
				<li><strong>Slope and drainage.</strong> Air-filled pipework condenses, and in a cold installation that condensate freezes where it collects. The runs must fall to drains, and every low point needs a drain that a technician can actually open and see discharge. Secondary and main drains are trim items on the order, not site scavenging.</li>
				<li><strong>Flush before the trim is connected.</strong> Construction grit is the leading cause of early pilot and trim failure on any diaphragm valve. Flush the mains, clear the strainers, then connect pilot tubing, solenoids and switches - the sequence is set out in the <a href="/guides/water-level-control-valve-guide/" class="text-brand-blue font-semibold hover:underline">hydraulic control valve commissioning section</a> and applies equally here.</li>
				<li><strong>Keep the air supply dry.</strong> A compressor feeding supervisory air without drying carries moisture into pipework that then freezes from the inside. Name the air quality you are supplying and the treatment fitted.</li>
			</ol>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">Freeze protection strategy belongs on the drawing before the order. Where the space stays cold, the pre-action or dry-pipe system is the protection; where the risk is an occasional frost in an otherwise heated building, the honest answer is insulation and heat trace on the vulnerable run rather than a valve change. The cold-climate behaviour of above-ground equipment is dealt with in the <a href="/guides/antifreeze-anti-collision-fire-hydrant-guide/" class="text-brand-blue font-semibold hover:underline">antifreeze and anti-collision hydrant guide</a>, and the mechanical reason a frozen wet section fails silently is the same there as here.</p>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Finally, connect the releasing circuit with the alarm interface documented: which terminals, which voltage, which supervision, and what the valve does when the panel loses power or a line is cut. A release circuit that fails open has converted a water-damage-prevention system into an uncontrolled deluge, and that behaviour should be a written acceptance item rather than an assumption.</p>

			<!-- Section 8 -->
			<h2 id="commissioning-and-periodic-testing" class="text-2xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-12 mb-4 tracking-tight scroll-mt-24">8. Commissioning and Periodic Testing</h2>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">Commission a pre-action installation in stages and record each one. The sequence that has served export projects well:</p>
			<ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2 mb-4">
				<li><strong>Water side:</strong> shell and seat proof of the delivered valve and its trim to the pressure stated on the datasheet - the reporting language is covered in the <a href="/guides/api-598-valve-pressure-test-guide/" class="text-brand-blue font-semibold hover:underline">pressure testing guide</a> - followed by a controlled trip test with the system drained and refillable.</li>
				<li><strong>Air tightness:</strong> charge to the supervisory band, record the make-up volume over a fixed interval, and accept against the leakage figure agreed in the specification. This single test predicts the running behaviour of the whole installation.</li>
				<li><strong>Interlock proof:</strong> test each path separately - detection only, sprinkler or vent only, both together, manual release - and record what the valve did in each case. On a double-interlock system the detection-only test must demonstrate that it does <strong>not</strong> admit water; that is the function being bought.</li>
				<li><strong>Delivery time:</strong> with the network charged with air, trip the system and time water at the most remote outlet. Compare with the specified figure.</li>
				<li><strong>Signals:</strong> confirm that low-air alarm, flow indication and valve position each reach the panel and are identifiable there.</li>
			</ul>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">In service, the in-service testing and maintenance document named in your contract sets the frequencies - NFPA 25 is the usual reference on US-spec work and EN or national equivalents elsewhere, with the edition to be confirmed from the specification rather than assumed. What matters commercially is that the air side is inspected on a real interval, because supervisory air leakage is the failure mode that produces both nuisance trips and the false confidence that a system is live when it is not.</p>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Keep the commissioning record with the valve. A five-year-old installation with its original tightness figures can be diagnosed from a change in make-up volume; the same installation without the record has to be drained, searched and re-tested at far greater cost.</p>

			<!-- Section 9 -->
			<h2 id="nuisance-trips-and-how-to-find-them" class="text-2xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-12 mb-4 tracking-tight scroll-mt-24">9. Nuisance Trips and How to Find Them</h2>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">Nuisance trips are the argument every facilities manager raises against pre-action, and almost all of them have identifiable causes. Work the list in order of frequency.</p>
			<ol class="list-decimal pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2 mb-4">
				<li><strong>Air leaking down.</strong> The symptom is a supervisory pressure that falls steadily and a maintenance device running often. Find the leak by section with the system charged, not by raising the set point - raising the set point only converts a slow leak into a trip at a higher pressure.</li>
				<li><strong>Frozen or blocked trim.</strong> A passage iced shut in winter behaves normally until the thaw, when the balance the valve holds changes and it trips. Trips on a rising morning temperature are diagnostic of this cause.</li>
				<li><strong>Water logged in the priming chamber.</strong> Check the drip and drain arrangement; a chamber that cannot breathe or drain gives erratic hold behaviour.</li>
				<li><strong>Release circuit faults.</strong> Loss of supervisory power, a failed coil, a shorted or open initiating line, or a panel programmed to the wrong fail-safe. Trip the electric path deliberately during commissioning so you know what it is supposed to do.</li>
				<li><strong>Debris in the pilot passages.</strong> The usual signature is a valve that holds badly, drips into the alarm path, or trips on a pressure oscillation elsewhere in the main. Strain the pilot supply and clean the passages rather than adjusting the set point to compensate.</li>
				<li><strong>Genuine demand.</strong> Occasionally a "nuisance" trip was the system working - a head that operated in a room nobody entered. Read the flow indicator zone and the panel history before writing it off.</li>
			</ol>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">False alarms without discharge are a different problem and usually belong to the alarm path: a <a href="/products/water-flow-indicator-zsjz/" class="text-brand-blue font-semibold hover:underline">flow indicator</a> whose delay is set too short will report a pressure surge as flow, and a wet system alarm path without a retard chamber will do the same mechanically. The <a href="/guides/water-flow-indicator-guide/" class="text-brand-blue font-semibold hover:underline">water flow indicator guide</a> covers that adjustment range.</p>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Related surge behaviour on the water side - including why a fast-closing valve upstream can look exactly like a faulty alarm valve - is covered in the <a href="/guides/water-hammer-protection-fire-water-systems/" class="text-brand-blue font-semibold hover:underline">water hammer protection guide</a>.</p>

			<!-- Section 10 -->
			<h2 id="pre-action-valve-rfq-checklist" class="text-2xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-12 mb-4 tracking-tight scroll-mt-24">10. Pre-action Valve RFQ Checklist</h2>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">Pre-action groups are quoted as assemblies, and the spread between two apparently identical offers is almost always in the trim. Put these lines in the enquiry.</p>
			<ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2 mb-4">
				<li><strong>Interlock arrangement.</strong> Single-interlock, double-interlock or dry-pipe duty - state which, and state the required behaviour when detection operates alone.</li>
				<li><strong>Size and class.</strong> DN80 - DN250 at 1.6 MPa on this group, plus the end connection and its drilling standard (see the <a href="/guides/flange-standard-guide-ansi-din-jis/" class="text-brand-blue font-semibold hover:underline">flange standard guide</a>).</li>
				<li><strong>Trigger method.</strong> Pneumatic, electric or manual, and for electric state coil voltage and supervision. The related ZSFM pilot is DC24V / 0.5A; the equivalent figure must be named on this enquiry too.</li>
				<li><strong>Air side scope.</strong> System-side supervisory pressure (0.03 - 0.05 MPa here), whether the pressure-maintaining and air-feeding devices are included, and whether the compressor or cylinder set is in your scope or ours.</li>
				<li><strong>Exhaust acceleration.</strong> State the specified maximum water delivery time, or state that there is none and accept the consequence in writing.</li>
				<li><strong>Alarm trim.</strong> Retard or delay chamber, water-motor gong, pressure switch contacts, gauges, test and drip connections.</li>
				<li><strong>Materials.</strong> Body and bonnet ductile iron QT450-10, clapper bronze or spheroidal iron with EPDM cover, reinforced NBR diaphragm, galvanized and copper-alloy trim - and name any deviation the specification requires.</li>
				<li><strong>Supervision devices.</strong> Isolation valve type with or without position signalling, and zone flow indicators by size and mounting (saddle, threaded, flanged or grooved).</li>
				<li><strong>Documentation.</strong> Shell and seat test records for the delivered size, material certificates, the general arrangement drawing with the trim shown, and the commissioning and spares list - the eight-point verification method is in the <a href="/guides/b2b-valve-sourcing-checklist/" class="text-brand-blue font-semibold hover:underline">sourcing checklist</a>.</li>
				<li><strong>Listing status.</strong> If the project requires listed equipment, say so at enquiry: listings belong to specific configurations and their holder must be named and verified, as the <a href="/guides/how-to-choose-ul-listed-fire-valves/" class="text-brand-blue font-semibold hover:underline">UL listed valve guide</a> explains.</li>
			</ul>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Assembling the pre-action valve with its heads, flow indicators, isolation valves and drains on one purchase order is where the saving actually appears - the <a href="/guides/commercial-building-sprinkler-system-bom-guide/" class="text-brand-blue font-semibold hover:underline">commercial sprinkler BOM guide</a> sets out that package line by line.</p>

